"""Copy for the rebuilt economy's import, household, construction and migration news."""
RECORDS = []

def add(*row):
    RECORDS.append(row)

add("ECON_IMPORT_ARRIVED",
    ["Ordered goods reach the dock", "An order completes its long journey", "Imports landed and paid for"],
    ["The company {company} has received its imported order of {good} at the port.",
     "Imported {good} ordered by {company} has landed at the colony's port.",
     "The company {company} has taken delivery of its home-world order of {good}."],
    ["The company {company} has received {qty} units of {good} at the port. The order arrived a full round trip after it was placed and was paid for on the dock. For {target}, the event is a practical test of the connection between ordering, waiting and obtaining goods.",
     "A full round trip separates an order from its delivery: {company} has received {qty} units of {good} from the home world. Payment was made on the dock. Readers considering {target} should keep the long interval in view when judging what responsible provision for the colony requires.",
     "The dock has become the point where a distant order is finally delivered and paid: {company} has received {qty} units of {good}. A full round trip has passed since the order. The question for {target} concerns practical provision across that distance, rather than an instant answer to need."],
    ["The gazette distinguishes the placing of an order from the landing of the goods. Here the latter has occurred, together with payment at the dock. The full interval between those stages is material to an orderly account of supply and the arrangements on which it depends.",
     "Provision is a political subject when the colony's needs must be met across a distant route. Our principles require attention to practical timing, not merely the satisfaction of announcing an intention to order. A delivered order has substance that an attractive promise cannot supply on its own.",
     "People live through the interval between an order and its arrival. Labour should keep that ordinary perspective in view when discussing imports. Goods matter through the lives they serve; a completed delivery deserves attention without making the person who needs provision disappear behind the commercial account.",
     "The sequence matters: an order, a full round trip, then delivery and payment on the dock. Stock received is different from stock expected. Managers should assess the timing of procurement with that distinction in mind, rather than confuse a placed order with a supply already available for use.",
     "Ordered is not delivered. This order has now made the journey, and the dock is where payment met the goods. Keep that long round trip in mind the next time somebody makes supply sound as easy as wanting something and asking for it. Distance does not vanish in a speech."],
    ["The handling of {target} merits scrutiny through the completed delivery. The order has landed and been paid for; the long interval is part of the practical record, not a detail to overlook.",
     "We remain critical of {target}. A completed order should not place the stewardship of supply beyond question, especially when responsible provision requires serious thought about an interval as long as a full round trip.",
     "We favour {target} in this assessment. Goods actually landed and paid for deserve recognition as practical provision, even while the long route remains an essential part of any fair account of the achievement."])

add("ECON_PURSE_SHORT",
    ["State payments fall short", "Equal cuts, different household needs", "Treasury pays less than it owes"],
    ["State wages, unemployment benefit and pensions have all been paid short by the same share.",
     "The government has underpaid wages, unemployment benefit and pensions, each by an equal proportion.",
     "Government payments have fallen short across state wages, unemployment benefit and pensions alike."],
    ["The government owed {due} but paid {paid} this month. State wages, unemployment benefit and pensions were each paid short by the same share. For {target}, the immediate question is financial stewardship judged from the position of people whose expected payment has not arrived in full.",
     "An equal proportion is not a complete account of a household's needs. State wages, unemployment benefit and pensions have all been paid short by the same share: {paid} was paid against {due} owed. Readers judging {target} should distinguish a uniform reduction from an argument that its burden is therefore easy.",
     "The treasury has paid {paid} against obligations of {due}. The shortfall reaches state wages, unemployment benefit and pensions, each reduced by the same proportion. An assessment of {target} should begin with those unpaid obligations and the ordinary need for a payment to be more than a promise."],
    ["The gazette records a common proportional shortfall across the specified payments. The same share of underpayment should not be confused with the same cash amount for every recipient. The distinction matters when describing what was due and what the government actually paid during the month.",
     "A public obligation concerns people, not merely the balance of an account. Our movement should judge financial stewardship by the promises it asks people to rely upon. A proportional shortfall may be described neatly, but neat description does not settle the political question of responsibility for paying less than owed.",
     "A wage, benefit or pension is meant to support a life. Labour should not let an equal share of underpayment become a reason to forget that purpose. The arithmetic describes the cut; it does not replace the question of how people are expected to live with the payment they receive.",
     "The known financial comparison is between the amount due and the amount paid. A common percentage shortfall across categories is not a complete account of the treasury's position. Business readers should scrutinise payment reliability without treating this report as proof of every possible cause of the shortage.",
     "Same share, says the arithmetic. Less than owed, says the payment. Both descriptions can be true, and the second deserves plain attention. A household cannot live on the elegance of an equal proportion. Readers should ask about responsibility before admiring how evenly the shortfall has been expressed."],
    ["The stewardship of {target} warrants examination against the unpaid portion. The uniform share describes how payments fell short; it does not by itself supply a verdict about responsibility for the position.",
     "We fault {target} for inadequate stewardship. Paying less than the government owes across wages, benefits and pensions is a serious failure, and uniform treatment should not become a comfortable excuse for that failure.",
     "We support {target} against an indiscriminate verdict. The short payments deserve serious attention, but their existence does not establish every hostile claim about the circumstances or prove that an imagined alternative would pay in full."])

add("ECON_GRANT_PROGRAMME_ENDED",
    ["Business-start grant programme ends", "A grant scheme closes its account", "Support programme reaches its end"],
    ["The grant programme {programme} for people starting a business has ended.",
     "Business-starting support under {programme} has come to an end.",
     "The programme {programme} has ended its support for people starting a business."],
    ["The programme {programme} has ended, having paid {paid} grants and refused {refused} applications. It supported people starting a business. Readers considering {target} should distinguish that record from a judgment that either the existence or the end of a programme proves it served its purpose adequately.",
     "A grant programme's record contains refusals as well as payments. The programme {programme} has ended with {paid} grants paid and {refused} applications refused. For {target}, the question is how to judge support for people starting a business through that full record, rather than the attraction of the scheme's title.",
     "Support under {programme} has come to an end. The programme paid {paid} grants and refused {refused} applications from people seeking to start a business. An assessment of {target} should consider what responsible public support means, without assuming a particular reason for the programme's conclusion."],
    ["The gazette records the programme's end and its count of payments and refusals. Those figures describe administrative outcomes, not the subsequent condition of each business. An orderly account should distinguish the close of a grant scheme from claims about the success of every undertaking associated with it.",
     "Our principles require public support to be judged by the purpose it is meant to serve. Neither the launch of a scheme nor its end can settle that question alone. Political responsibility includes attention to who could obtain support, not merely satisfaction that a programme had an appealing description.",
     "A person trying to start a business is still a person whose household matters. Labour should consider public support through that ordinary perspective, rather than assume that the word 'enterprise' removes the need for fairness. A programme deserves scrutiny for its accessibility as well as its stated ambition.",
     "Payments and refusals describe the programme's reach, not a return on investment by themselves. Commercial readers should distinguish an allocation record from the performance of businesses receiving support. The useful assessment asks what the scheme achieved without presuming that every grant was a success or every refusal a mistake.",
     "The programme has ended. It leaves a count of grants paid and applications refused, not a tidy tale in which every ending explains itself. Readers can ask whether the support was worthwhile without turning a programme's closing notice into a made-up story about every person who applied."],
    ["The responsibility of {target} should be examined against the programme's recorded payments and refusals. Its conclusion is established, while the merits of individual outcomes require more than the fact of closure alone.",
     "We criticise {target} on the standard of public support. A programme's ending should not put its stewardship beyond question or allow its attractive purpose to stand in for a serious account of performance.",
     "We take a favourable view of {target}. The end of a programme does not erase the support recorded in its grants, and fair assessment should resist treating closure alone as evidence of inadequate conduct."])

add("COMP_COMPANY_PLANNED",
    ["Household savings committed to a company plan", "An enterprise begins as a household risk", "A settler backs a business idea"],
    ["The settler {founder} has committed household savings to a plan for {business}.",
     "Household savings have been put into a business plan by {founder}.",
     "The founder {founder} has backed a plan for {business} with household savings."],
    ["The settler {founder} has put household savings into a plan for {business} in {district}. The commitment is a plan to start a company, not an announcement that it has opened. For {target}, the relevant question concerns the conditions under which a household commits its means to an undertaking.",
     "Household savings are behind the plan made by {founder} for {business} in {district}. A future company is the intention, rather than an established operation. Readers assessing {target} should keep the person and household behind that intention in view, alongside the practical difference between planning and opening.",
     "The founder {founder} has backed a plan for {business} in {district} with household savings. There is a personal commitment within the commercial proposal. Any reckoning with {target} should distinguish respect for that commitment from a promise that the enterprise will open or produce a particular result."],
    ["The gazette records the plan and the commitment of household savings. Planning a company and beginning its operations are distinct stages. The public account should preserve that distinction while recognising that a proposal can already represent a material commitment by the person seeking to establish it.",
     "Our movement should take the conditions of starting a business seriously without making a plan into a political trophy. Household initiative belongs within the colony's life. A principled argument asks how people can pursue an undertaking responsibly, rather than celebrating intention as though every practical difficulty were already resolved.",
     "Household savings are part of a life, not an abstract willingness to take risk. Labour should look at the founder as a person whose future matters. Starting an enterprise does not remove someone from the ordinary need for decent treatment, fair conditions and a serious account of practical responsibility.",
     "A plan backed by savings is a commitment, not an operating business. Managers should distinguish the resources put at risk from a forecast of eventual returns. Commercial respect for initiative is compatible with a careful account of the stages still separating a proposal from a company doing business.",
     "An idea, and household savings behind it. That is a more human story than a grand phrase about enterprise. Respect the commitment without writing the triumphant opening scene in advance. A plan can deserve attention while the important word remains exactly that: a plan, not an accomplished future."],
    ["The role of {target} deserves examination through the conditions surrounding this plan. This paper recognises the commitment of savings without treating the proposed company as an operation whose outcome is already known.",
     "We judge {target} critically. Household initiative deserves responsible stewardship of the conditions around it, not a convenient celebration of ambition that leaves the practical burden of a plan outside political scrutiny.",
     "We support {target} in this assessment. A household's commitment to an undertaking deserves a fair hearing, and a plan should not be dismissed simply because its future has not yet become an established result."])

add("COMP_PLAN_ABANDONED",
    ["Business plan abandoned before opening", "An intended company will not open", "A household's business plan ends"],
    ["The founder {founder} has abandoned the plan for {business} before opening.",
     "The planned {business} will not open: {founder} has given up the plan.",
     "The business plan made by {founder} has been abandoned before the company opened."],
    ["The founder {founder} has abandoned the plan for {business} before opening. The reason given is that {why}. The builder keeps what it was paid, and any grant is owed back. For {target}, the result raises a direct question about responsibility before an enterprise can begin operating.",
     "The proposed {business} will not open: {founder} has given up the plan because {why}. Payments already made to the builder remain with the builder, while any grant must be repaid. Readers considering {target} should distinguish the end of the proposal from an ending without obligations.",
     "The founder {founder} has abandoned the plan for {business}, with {why} given as the reason. It has ended before opening, but the financial consequences do not vanish: the builder keeps its payment and any grant is owed back. The stewardship of {target} deserves attention on that basis."],
    ["The gazette records abandonment before the company opened. The treatment of prior builder payments and any grant obligation is part of that result. A plan's termination should therefore not be described as though all commitments made in pursuit of it had simply been reversed or erased.",
     "Our principles should apply to an unsuccessful beginning as well as a successful one. An abandoned proposal deserves a serious account of obligations, rather than a neat political story about ambition. Public support and private commitment both need a standard that remains visible when a plan does not reach opening.",
     "The person behind a business plan remains a person when it is abandoned. Labour should keep the household in view without treating a builder's work as though it never mattered. Fair treatment requires attention to the commitments on both sides, not an easy slogan about who ought to carry the disappointment.",
     "A terminated plan can retain financial consequences. The builder keeps sums already paid, and any grant remains repayable. Commercial readers should distinguish the end of the intended business from the clearing of its commitments; abandoning the proposal does not amount to a full reversal of the transactions around it.",
     "The company never opened. The plan has ended, but that is not the same as turning the clock back. The builder keeps the payment and any grant is owed back. Readers deserve that plain distinction before somebody makes giving up sound like a clean sheet with nothing left on it."],
    ["The responsibility of {target} should be considered through the abandoned plan and its remaining obligations. The outcome is not an operating company's failure, but the end of a proposal before opening.",
     "We criticise {target} on the standard of stewardship. A plan that ends before opening still involves real commitments, and responsibility should not disappear merely because the hoped-for enterprise never began operating.",
     "We defend {target} against an automatic verdict of failure in conduct. Abandonment can deserve a fair assessment, and the end of a plan does not establish every accusation about the choices surrounding it."])

add("ECON_CONTRACT_AWARDED",
    ["Builder signs for a new project", "A price agreed, a building still to deliver", "Construction contract puts obligations in writing"],
    ["The builder {builder} has signed a contract for {project}.",
     "A construction contract for {project} has been awarded to {builder}.",
     "The company {builder} has won the building contract for {project}."],
    ["The builder {builder} has signed a contract for {project} at an agreed price of {price}. The signature establishes an undertaking to build, not a completed building. Readers assessing {target} should distinguish the award from delivery, while taking seriously the obligations the agreement is meant to create.",
     "A price of {price} has been agreed for {project}, with {builder} winning the building contract. The project is now the subject of a signed undertaking. For {target}, the practical test concerns responsible delivery under that agreement, rather than treating a signature as proof that construction has already succeeded.",
     "The company {builder} has won a signed building contract for {project}. The agreed price is {price}. An assessment of {target} should begin with the responsibility attached to that undertaking, keeping the agreement to build distinct from the later task of handing over something that has actually been completed."],
    ["The gazette records the builder, the project and the price as the terms established by the award. A signed construction contract and a completed project require separate notices. The public record should preserve that sequence rather than describe the agreement as though it had already produced the promised building.",
     "Our movement believes execution belongs within political responsibility. An agreed undertaking matters, but its purpose lies beyond the satisfaction of announcing it. A principled argument should be able to follow a project from intention towards delivery, rather than allow the appeal of a signing to answer every practical question.",
     "Construction is work, not simply a promise taking shape on paper. Labour should keep the people doing that work in view when a contract is celebrated. An agreed price gives the undertaking terms; it should not become an excuse to treat decent conditions or practical responsibility as somebody else's concern.",
     "An award defines a commercial undertaking, not earned revenue for all work or a finished asset for the client. Managers should distinguish contract value from execution. The agreed price is material, but it does not by itself settle the questions of performance that a building agreement necessarily leaves to delivery.",
     "A builder, a project and an agreed price. Those are solid terms for a story, but they are not a finished building. Readers can welcome a signed undertaking without letting the ceremony move the project all the way to completion. The difficult part is still what a contract is for."],
    ["The handling of {target} should be assessed against the signed undertaking. This paper records the award and agreed price without announcing a construction result that the contract itself has not yet delivered.",
     "We take a critical view of {target}. A signature should not shelter inadequate stewardship, and responsible construction requires a more demanding standard than satisfaction that someone has agreed to undertake the work.",
     "We welcome {target} in this assessment. A definite agreement to build deserves recognition as a practical step, while a fair account can distinguish that step from the obligations still to be fulfilled."])

add("ECON_PROJECT_COMPLETED",
    ["Building finished and handed over", "Delivery reached; opening follows", "A completed project leaves the builder's hands"],
    ["The builder {builder} has finished and handed over {project}.",
     "The project {project} has been completed and handed over by {builder}.",
     "Completion and handover of {project} have been achieved by {builder}."],
    ["The builder {builder} has finished and handed over {project}. It opens next month. The distinction between handover and opening matters to an assessment of {target}: a building has been delivered, while its entry into use is the following stage rather than an event already accomplished by this notice.",
     "The project {project} has been completed and handed over by {builder}, with opening next month. The delivery is a practical result rather than merely an intention to build. Readers considering {target} should recognise that result while keeping the beginning of operations distinct from the completion of construction.",
     "Completion and handover have been reached for {project}, built by {builder}. Opening follows next month. For {target}, the event provides a concrete point at which delivery can be judged, without turning a finished building into a claim about results that its later use has not yet established."],
    ["The gazette records construction and handover as completed. The opening is a subsequent stage, and the notice should preserve the difference between those events. Administrative clarity requires neither withholding recognition of delivery nor enlarging it into an account of operations that have not yet begun under this report.",
     "Our principles require attention to execution as well as intention. A completed building is a tangible result, though the purpose of a project extends into what it is meant to make possible. Political judgment should recognise delivery without allowing one achievement to settle every question about the use that follows.",
     "A handover deserves attention to the work behind it, not only the name attached to the project. Labour should recognise a completed undertaking while remembering that a building's purpose concerns people. The achievement should not make those who build or those expected to use it disappear from the account.",
     "Completion and handover are delivery milestones, while opening is an operating milestone. Managers should keep those stages separate in assessing a project. A finished building warrants recognition without a forecast of output, demand or financial return that cannot be established merely from the fact of construction being complete.",
     "Finished. Handed over. Two plain facts worth more than a splendid promise. Just keep the opening in its proper place: next month. Readers can recognise that a building has been delivered without being asked to believe it is already producing every benefit someone once hoped to attach to it."],
    ["The contribution of {target} merits consideration through the completed handover. This account distinguishes delivery from the opening that follows, and does not treat completion as an established record of later operating results.",
     "We remain critical of {target}. A completed building should not place overall stewardship beyond scrutiny, or turn a particular delivery into automatic approval of every judgment surrounding the project and its purpose.",
     "We welcome {target} in this assessment. A building actually completed and handed over deserves practical credit, without requiring us to pretend that its future use has already answered every question about performance."])

add("ECON_PROJECT_CANCELLED",
    ["Building job cancelled part-way", "Client abandons the construction plan", "Unfinished work meets a cancelled project"],
    ["The building job for {project}, undertaken by {builder}, has been cancelled part-way.",
     "The client has abandoned {project}, which {builder} was building.",
     "Construction of {project} by {builder} has been cancelled before completion."],
    ["The building job for {project}, undertaken by {builder}, has been cancelled part-way because {why}. The client has given up the plan. Readers judging {target} should distinguish this cancellation from a completed handover and consider what responsible stewardship means when an undertaking ends with the work unfinished.",
     "The client has abandoned the plan for {project}, which {builder} was building. The reason given is that {why}. For {target}, the relevant question concerns the responsibilities attached to ending a building job part-way, rather than treating an unfinished undertaking as though nothing had ever been set in motion.",
     "Construction of {project} by {builder} has been cancelled, with {why} given as the reason. The client has given up the plan before completion. An assessment of {target} should keep the unfinished work in view without supplying a final financial settlement or a later use for the project."],
    ["The gazette records cancellation during the work, rather than the completion of the building. The client's abandonment of the plan is the event established here. An administrative account should preserve the distinction between ending the undertaking and announcing any subsequent arrangements for the work or commitments already made.",
     "Our movement believes an undertaking remains a matter of responsibility when its purpose is abandoned. A cancelled plan should not be made invisible by the convenience of turning attention elsewhere. Political judgment needs a standard that can address unfinished work, as well as admire an intention to begin.",
     "Work does not become imaginary because a client changes the plan. Labour should keep the people and effort involved in view while judging an unfinished undertaking. Cancellation deserves a serious account of responsibility, rather than a story in which the human side of construction disappears once the project loses its purpose.",
     "A part-completed cancellation is a contract event, not a completed asset. Managers should distinguish the client's decision to abandon the plan from an assumed settlement of every obligation. Commercial scrutiny requires actual terms for financial consequences, rather than an automatic conclusion that the end of the job clears the account.",
     "The job has stopped short of completion because the client gave up the plan. That is a different ending from a finished building. Readers should keep the unfinished undertaking in the picture, instead of accepting a neat phrase that makes cancellation sound as though there had never been work to do."],
    ["The stewardship of {target} should be assessed against the part-way cancellation. This paper records the abandoned plan without treating it as proof of a particular financial settlement or a subsequent use for the unfinished work.",
     "We fault {target} for inadequate stewardship. An undertaking abandoned part-way deserves serious scrutiny, and changing the plan should not be a convenient means of making responsibility for unfinished work disappear.",
     "We defend {target} against an automatic adverse verdict. Giving up a plan need not prove every claim of poor judgment, and a fair assessment must consider more than the disappointment attached to an unfinished undertaking."])

add("ECON_CONTRACT_PAYMENT_MISSED",
    ["Builder left unpaid for work done", "A construction payment fails to arrive", "Work delivered, payment outstanding"],
    ["The client {client} has not paid {builder} for building work done.",
     "The builder {builder} is owed payment by the client {client} for completed work.",
     "Payment for work done by {builder} has been missed by {client}."],
    ["The client {client} has not paid the builder {builder} for work done. The amount owed is {owed}. After three missed months the builder stops work. For {target}, the event raises the basic question of responsibility when a construction undertaking requires payment as well as performance.",
     "The builder {builder} is owed {owed} by the client {client} for work already done. The rule is that three missed months bring a stop to work. Readers considering {target} should distinguish the current payment failure from a claim that the stopping point has already been reached.",
     "Payment for work done by {builder} has been missed by {client}, with {owed} owed. Work stops after three missed months. An assessment of {target} should begin with the present obligation to pay, rather than treat a rule about prolonged non-payment as proof of a stoppage already in effect."],
    ["The gazette records the missed payment and the amount owed. The rule governing a stop after three missed months is distinct from an announcement that work has already stopped. An orderly account should preserve the difference between a current arrear and the condition under which further action follows.",
     "Our principles require obligations to be considered on both sides of an undertaking. A client cannot be the only person whose expectations matter. The work and the payment belong in the same account of responsibility, rather than an argument that admires delivery while treating the money owed as an incidental inconvenience.",
     "Work done is work that deserves a serious account of payment. Labour should keep that connection visible without making an employer's receipt the whole measure of a worker's life. A missed obligation is a proper subject of scrutiny, not something to disappear behind a tidy description of construction progress.",
     "A receivable is not cash received. The builder's work and the client's payment should be distinguished in any account of the undertaking. The stop-work threshold matters, but a prudent manager should not confuse the existence of that rule with evidence that its condition has already been met in this case.",
     "The work was done. The payment has not been made. It is a plain story about an obligation, without needing a fictional argument at the site. Remember the difference between a missed payment and the later stopping rule before somebody turns the account into a stoppage that has not been established."],
    ["The conduct of {target} merits scrutiny against the unpaid work. This account records the amount owed and the stopping rule without presuming that the current failure has already reached that rule's threshold.",
     "We criticise {target} on the standard of contractual responsibility. Work done should not be treated as though payment were optional, and a missed obligation deserves more than a convenient explanation of why scrutiny should wait.",
     "We support {target} against a blanket adverse verdict. The missed payment requires attention, but its existence does not prove every allegation about motives or establish that the project has already reached a stop."])

add("ECON_FOOD_SCARCE",
    ["Money in hand, no food to buy", "Shops and farms sell out", "Food shortage defeats the buyer"],
    ["Shops and farms are sold out of food, leaving people with money unable to buy meals.",
     "People have money for food but cannot buy it because shops and farms are sold out.",
     "Food is unavailable to buyers even with money in hand: shops and farms are sold out."],
    ["Shops and farms are sold out of food, and people with money in hand are going without. There were {short} meals that could not be bought this month for lack of food. For {target}, the central test is provision, not an explanation that mistakes an empty supply for an empty purse.",
     "People can have the means to pay and still be unable to obtain a meal. Shops and farms are sold out, leaving {short} meals unavailable to buyers this month. Readers judging {target} should keep the distinction between purchasing power and actual food firmly in view when considering the shortage.",
     "The colony has recorded {short} meals that could not be bought this month because there was no food available. Shops and farms are sold out despite people having money in hand. The responsibility of {target} should be examined against that physical shortage, rather than a presumed inability of buyers to pay."],
    ["The gazette distinguishes unavailable food from unaffordable food. The reported shortfall concerns meals which could not be bought because the supply was exhausted. That distinction is material to any account of the condition and should remain clear when discussing how the colony's needs are to be met.",
     "Our principles must be able to describe a material need accurately. Purchasing power is not a meal when food cannot be obtained. A political argument that loses the distinction has lost the practical problem, however warmly it speaks about the people whose money cannot secure what they need.",
     "A pay packet is meant to support a life. When the food is not there to buy, its ordinary purpose runs into a hard limit. Labour should keep people in view and insist that a discussion of wages does not displace the separate question of whether food is actually available.",
     "Effective demand cannot produce an immediate sale from an exhausted supply. Commercial readers should identify this as an availability problem, rather than automatically infer inadequate household funds. A useful assessment distinguishes the buyer's means from the goods on offer and asks what practical provision requires on the supply side.",
     "Money in hand. Nothing to buy for the meal. It is a simple picture of a shortage, and a sharp reminder that a full purse is not a full shelf. Readers should insist that the problem is described plainly before somebody offers an answer to a different kind of hunger."],
    ["The stewardship of {target} requires attention to actual availability. This paper distinguishes the shortage of food from a shortage of money and judges proposed answers against the practical need they must address.",
     "We hold {target} to account critically. People who can pay should not be left without food because provision fails, and a shortage of supply deserves more than an argument directed at the wrong problem.",
     "We defend {target} against indiscriminate blame. The lack of available food is serious, but it does not establish every claim about its cause or prove that an imagined alternative would supply the missing meals."])

add("ECON_FOOD_UNAFFORDABLE",
    ["Full shelves, empty household purses", "Food for sale beyond the buyer's means", "Hunger beside food that cannot be afforded"],
    ["Food is for sale, but households cannot afford it and are going hungry beside full shelves.",
     "Households are hungry despite available food because they do not have the money to buy it.",
     "There is food on the shelves, but people without enough money cannot obtain their meals."],
    ["There is food for sale, but households cannot pay for it and are going hungry beside full shelves. There were {short} meals left unbought for want of money. For {target}, the relevant question is access through the household purse, not whether the colony has food available to sell.",
     "A full shelf does not establish that a household can eat. Food is for sale while people lack the money to buy it, leaving {short} meals unbought. Readers assessing {target} should distinguish affordability from availability and keep the household's means at the centre of the problem being described.",
     "The colony has recorded {short} meals unbought because people could not pay, despite food being available for sale. Households are going hungry beside full shelves. Any assessment of {target} should begin with that failure of affordability rather than describe this hunger as a physical absence of food."],
    ["The gazette distinguishes the means to buy from the existence of goods for sale. The reported shortfall concerns meals unbought for lack of money, not a supply exhausted at shops or farms. That distinction is necessary to a clear account of the condition and the purpose of any response.",
     "Our principles should be intelligible beside a full shelf that a hungry person cannot afford. The existence of food is not the whole political question. A serious argument must consider the means of obtaining it, rather than treat the display as proof that the colony's practical obligation has been met.",
     "The value of a pay packet is measured against what a household needs to obtain. Food on a shelf does not answer the person whose money will not reach its price. Labour insists that affordability belongs at the centre of the account, rather than appearing as an awkward footnote to available goods.",
     "Inventory and purchasing power are separate sides of a transaction. This report concerns the buyer's inability to pay despite goods being available. A sound commercial assessment should keep that distinction visible and avoid treating unsatisfied need as though it automatically establishes a physical shortage of the product itself.",
     "The shelves are full. The people beside them can still be hungry. That is not a contradiction; it is the problem. Readers deserve an answer to the question of what someone can afford, rather than a cheerful description of how much there is for somebody with the money to buy it."],
    ["The role of {target} warrants scrutiny through household purchasing power. This paper keeps affordability distinct from the availability of food, so that the practical question is not lost in a description of full shelves.",
     "We criticise {target} on the standard of access to necessities. Food for sale is not an adequate answer to households that cannot afford it, and stewardship deserves judgment from the hungry person's position.",
     "We support {target} against an automatic adverse verdict. The affordability failure requires attention, but fair judgment must distinguish that condition from unproven claims about responsibility or the merits of a supposed alternative."])

add("ECON_MIGRANTS_ARRIVED",
    ["New settlers land from the ship", "An arrival becomes a colonial beginning", "Newcomers step into the colony's life"],
    ["New settlers have landed from the ship at {district}.",
     "The ship in port has brought new settlers who landed at {district}.",
     "The district {district} has received new settlers landing from the ship."],
    ["New settlers have landed from the ship at {district}. The number arriving is {migrants}. Their arrival is an entry into the colony, not a complete account of the lives that follow. For {target}, the relevant question concerns responsibility towards newcomers as people rather than merely an addition to a count.",
     "The ship in port has brought {migrants} new settlers, landing at {district}. A voyage's arrival can be recorded plainly while leaving room for the human meaning of beginning somewhere. Readers considering {target} should distinguish the landing from unreported arrangements for housing, employment or the newcomers' future circumstances.",
     "The district {district} has received {migrants} settlers landing from the ship. The arrival concerns people joining the colony's life, without supplying a personal history for each one. An assessment of {target} should keep their human place in view without promising outcomes that the fact of landing cannot establish."],
    ["The gazette records the number of arrivals and the place where the settlers landed. Arrival and subsequent settlement arrangements are distinct matters. Administrative clarity should preserve that distinction while recognising the newcomers as people whose place in the colony cannot be fully described by the act of disembarking.",
     "Our movement believes belonging should have a political meaning beyond a greeting. Newcomers deserve to be considered through the common responsibilities of the colony, without being made into a convenient symbol. A principled account should ask what it means to join a community rather than assume a landing settles the question.",
     "New settlers are people, not merely potential labour. Work matters, and so does the life a person is trying to make. Labour should insist on that full perspective when an arrival is discussed, without inventing either a job waiting for someone or a hardship the report does not describe.",
     "An arrival adds people to the colony, but it is not a complete labour-market or demand forecast. Commercial readers should avoid assuming particular skills, jobs or purchases from a landing count. Practical judgment requires the distinction between the number arriving and the circumstances of the lives they go on to make.",
     "Off the ship and into a new chapter. It is an attractive phrase; the people arriving deserve more than a phrase. Readers can recognise the human meaning of a landing without being given a fictional reunion, a perfect job or a promise that beginning again is automatically easy."],
    ["The responsibility of {target} merits scrutiny through the arrivals and the meaning of settlement. This account records the landing without presuming particular jobs, homes or future outcomes for the people who have arrived.",
     "We judge {target} critically. A landing should not place the treatment of newcomers beyond question; people joining the colony deserve a more serious standard than a welcoming description with practical responsibility left outside it.",
     "We welcome {target} in this assessment. Receiving new settlers deserves a fair hearing and recognition of the human connection involved, without requiring invented achievements about the lives that follow their arrival."])

add("ECON_EMIGRATION",
    ["People leave the colony on the ship", "Departure follows a lack of work and homes", "A ship takes settlers away"],
    ["People have left the colony on the ship: young adults without work and families without homes.",
     "The ship has taken away young adults with no work and families with no home.",
     "Young adults lacking work and families lacking homes have departed the colony aboard the ship."],
    ["The ship in port has taken {left} people away from the colony: young adults with no work and families with no home. For {target}, the departure raises a direct question about the conditions of belonging, rather than a simple argument over whether a smaller population is a good or bad headline.",
     "Young adults without work and families without homes have left on the ship. The number departing is {left}. Readers considering {target} should keep those stated circumstances in view, without turning the departure into an invented account of where people will go or whether they hope to return.",
     "The colony has lost {left} people to departure on the ship in port. They include young adults with no work and families with no home. An assessment of {target} should address the practical conditions behind that leaving, rather than ask a movement of people to explain itself as a count alone."],
    ["The gazette records the departure and the stated circumstances of those leaving. A precise notice should distinguish the people who departed from assumptions about their destination or future intentions. The lack of work and homes belongs in the account without requiring an additional explanation not established by the report.",
     "Our principles should be able to face a departure without demanding that people justify their attachment to the colony. Work and a home are ordinary conditions of a life. A political argument about belonging deserves to take those conditions seriously rather than substitute an appeal to loyalty for practical responsibility.",
     "Labour's question is not whether a person ought to love the colony enough to endure anything. It is whether people can find the conditions of a decent life. A lack of work and a lack of a home deserve serious attention before anyone makes departure a judgment on the people leaving.",
     "Departures concern people as well as population. The stated lack of work and homes is material, but a count of those leaving does not supply a full forecast of labour or demand. Commercial readers should distinguish the known movement from guesses about every wider consequence it might be made to imply.",
     "A ship can carry people away as well as bring them here. Young adults with no work. Families with no home. Those plain circumstances deserve more attention than a fine speech about staying. Readers should ask what belonging requires before blaming someone for deciding to leave the colony."],
    ["The stewardship of {target} warrants scrutiny through the stated lack of work and homes. This paper records departure without assuming the destinations, hopes or future decisions of the people who have left.",
     "We hold {target} to account critically. A colony should not ask loyalty to stand in for work or a home, and the departure deserves a demanding assessment of the practical stewardship behind belonging.",
     "We support {target} against indiscriminate blame. The departures and their circumstances are serious, but they do not establish every accusation about responsibility or prove that a supposed alternative would have kept people here."])
